From 5680d13c7488501804a18bc67187880fb2b384f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 10:42:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 352/352] v2.2.4: harden the rtrafd health probe so it's light
 enough to leave on

The opt-in per-device health probe (rtraf_hprobe, off by default) did too much
work on single 10 Hz collector ticks: a whole /proc/net/nf_conntrack parse with an
O(NCLI=192) inner scan, a 192-wide raw-ICMP burst, and a blocking flash append -
which stuttered the live analyzer and, via a ksoftirqd (SCHED_RR) softirq storm,
tripped RT throttling during a speed test. v2.2.3 fixed the probe-INDEPENDENT
residual (O(1) cli_slot); this fixes the probe-DEPENDENT load so the feature is
safe to actually enable.

Health cycle is now a phased state machine (IDLE->SCAN->SEND->WAIT), a bounded
slice per base tick:
  - SCAN: amortize the conntrack parse at HCONN_PER_TICK=256 lines/tick, with a new
    O(1) fnv(ip)->slot index (hp_ipindex_*) replacing the per-line O(NCLI) walk.
    Live tcp_total/tcp_estab publish only on a COMPLETE pass (scratch tcp_next/te_next),
    never partial.
  - SEND: spread echoes at HSEND_PER_TICK=16/tick; per-device tsend stamped at the
    real send instant so spreading doesn't distort RTT.
  - Retention: write_health() now spools the JSONL history row to a bounded RAM file
    (/tmp/rtraf/healthhist.spool); the actual flash append folds into the existing
    hourly db_save via health_flush_spool() - zero NAND/USB I/O on the hot loop.
  - Back-off: a new cycle won't start while either WAN direction exceeds HBUSY_BPS
    (300 Mbps), so the sweep never competes with a speed test.
  - Default rtraf_hint 30 -> 60 s. A new cycle only starts from IDLE, so a long
    cycle delays the next instead of overlapping.

No behavior change when rtraf_hprobe=0 (the default). Metal validation owed; keep
rtraf_hprobe=0 on v2.2.3 until this is field-confirmed. See docs/V222-REGRESSION-PLAN.md.

BE96U MCP built (MAKE_EXIT=0, rtrafd.o fresh, warning-clean) for owner testing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
